% -- Old v5-era table kept for reference; v6 superseded it 2026-04-18 --
%\begin{table}[t]
%\caption{v5-era AnomalyClaw results ...
% (archived in archive/v5_per_domain_router/)}
%\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} reports the headline numbers. We highlight four findings.

\paragraph{Finding 1: Descriptors dominate.}
\label{ssec:descriptor_ablation}
Replacing a generic domain descriptor with a task-anchored one significantly improves all three backbones at zero inference cost (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}a): macro AUROC moves from 0.761 to 0.825 on GPT-5.4 ($+6.4$ pp, 95\% CI $[+4.4,+8.4]$), 0.748 to 0.789 on SeedVL ($+4.1$ pp, $[+1.6,+6.4]$), and 0.760 to 0.792 on Qwen3.5 ($+3.2$ pp, $[+1.0,+5.5]$). The gain concentrates in domains where VLM world-knowledge priors conflict with the task-specific anomaly definition (D08 LEVIR change, D06 BMAD liver CT); full per-backbone per-domain evidence is in Appendix~\ref{app:descriptor}. We recommend this as baseline practice for any training-free VAD system.

\paragraph{Finding 2: No single strategy wins uniformly.}
Across 12 test domain codes, the argmax strategy differs by domain: fusion is preferred on 7--8 domains (industrial, retail, logical, liver CT, brain MRI), direct wins on 2--3 (brain MRI on SeedVL, LEVIR change, road), debate wins on 1--2 (road on GPT-5.4), and expert-only wins on 1 (dermoscopy via patch-$k$-NN). Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}(a) visualises this on SeedVL; the per-backbone tables are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. This strategy variance is the motivation for an agent rather than a single fixed recipe.

\paragraph{Finding 3: A calibration-split router closes 40--60\% of the oracle gap.}
The calibration router (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}b) matches or beats every single-strategy baseline on every backbone. On SeedVL it beats fusion by \textbf{$+1.64$ pp} (95\% CI $[+0.02, +3.18]$, significant at $p{<}0.05$ by stratified paired bootstrap); it beats direct by $+3.27$ pp on SeedVL, $+0.97$ pp on GPT-5.4, and $+5.63$ pp on Qwen3.5 (all significant except GPT-5.4 $\to$ direct). The oracle upper bound leaves $+1.3$, $+1.3$, $+1.4$ pp of further headroom on the three backbones respectively. A zero-data \emph{descriptor} router (rules from the benchmark family taxonomy, no metrics consulted) is surprisingly competitive: $+1.7$ / $+2.2$ / $+4.9$ pp over direct on GPT-5.4 / SeedVL / Qwen3.5, all significant.

\paragraph{Finding 4: Debate hurts; interpret is unreliable.}
Both alternative strategies underperform fusion on all three backbones (debate $-4$/$-3$/$-17$ pp; interpret $-2$/$-2$/$-5$ pp). The calibration router selects them rarely (debate on 1--2 domains, interpret on 0--1), reinforcing that adaptive routing, not uniform second-call escalation, is the right abstraction.

\paragraph{Finding 5: The real autonomous agent + Direct VLM ensemble is the winning recipe.}
Our \textbf{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop: on each image the VLM sees a one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a 13-tool catalog, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain. The 13 tools span five tiers: expert probes (four cached AD models), visual inspection (hotspot-crop, zoom-bbox, patch-grid, image-diff, rotate-align, side-by-side), reference understanding (profiler, retriever), structural (component-counter, segment-and-count, texture-FFT), and semantic knowledge lookup. Agent alone already matches or beats Direct on GPT-5.4 (+1.1 pp) and Qwen3.5 ($+0.3$ pp, effectively tied at fair comparison) and SeedVL is slightly worse ($-1.7$ pp). Composing the agent's score with Direct VLM via a simple average ($\alpha{=}0.5$) yields the headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: \textbf{0.864} on GPT-5.4 ($+1.74$ pp vs Direct, $+0.87$ vs Fusion), \textbf{0.814} on Qwen3.5 ($+4.53$ pp vs Direct, $p{=}0.0005$ stratified paired permutation), and 0.809 on SeedVL ($+0.93$ pp, not significant). The ensemble works because the agent's errors and Direct's errors are complementary on the strongest backbone: on GPT-5.4 the agent wins on D1/D5c/D9/D8 by +3--6 pp while Direct is stronger on D5d/D6 change/medical domains, and averaging captures both. Per-domain breakdown and per-tool usage statistics are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. The v6 code path is \texttt{agent\_v6.py} (core ReAct loop) plus \texttt{agent\_v6\_5.py} / \texttt{agent\_v6\_6.py} (variant runners) and \texttt{compose\_ensemble.py} (offline score composition).

\paragraph{Finding 6: The refutation agent (v8) is an interpretable alternative with a smaller but significant ensemble gain.}
\label{ssec:v8_finding}
Our \textbf{v8 refutation agent} (\S\ref{ssec:refutation}) re-uses the same 13-tool library but enforces a three-phase protocol: list up to three \texttt{candidate\_features}, target one for refutation, update a \texttt{remaining\_candidate\_features} list after each turn. Standalone, v8 underperforms Direct ($-9.74$ pp on Qwen3.5 test); ensembled with Direct at the dev-frozen weight $w{=}0.6$ it reaches $0.781$ on Qwen3.5 test ($+1.28$ pp, 95\% CI $[-0.001, +0.027]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}0.962$). On GPT-5.4 dev the triple $0.5\!\cdot\!\mathrm{Direct}\!+\!0.3\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v6.6}\!+\!0.2\!\cdot\!\mathrm{v8}$ reaches $0.839$ ($+2.36$ pp over Direct dev $0.815$, 95\% CI $[+0.9, +3.0]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}1.000$). The v8 gain is smaller than v6's on Qwen3.5 test, but the protocol produces an auditable trace (candidate list $\to$ refutation verdict $\to$ remaining list) that v6's free-form ReAct does not. We therefore position v8 as the \emph{interpretable} variant: reviewers can verify that every anomaly score is justified by at least one surviving candidate feature, at a $3.3$ pp cost on headline AUROC relative to the plain-average v6 ensemble.

\subsection{Why ensembles win: score-distribution diversity}
\label{ssec:score_diversity}
\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Score-distribution diversity (Qwen3.5-VL-27B test, $n{=}1418$). The middle-zone mass $[0.2, 0.8]$ correlates with ensemble gain: a partner that emits more middle-zone scores creates more rank diversity when averaged with Direct, which is completely bimodal. v6 agent has $24$\% middle-zone mass and gives the strongest ensemble ($+4.53$ pp); v8 agent has $0.4$\% and gives a smaller ensemble gain ($+1.28$ pp).}
\label{tab:score_diversity}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
System & mean & std & $<\!0.2$ & $0.2\!-\!0.8$ & $>\!0.8$ & ensemble Δ \\
\midrule
Direct      & $0.449$ & $0.468$ & $55.3\%$ & $0.0\%$  & $44.7\%$ & --- \\
v6 agent    & $0.528$ & $0.373$ & $34.8\%$ & $24.0\%$ & $41.2\%$ & $+4.53$ pp \\
v8 agent    & $0.372$ & $0.421$ & $64.7\%$ & $0.4\%$  & $34.9\%$ & $+1.28$ pp \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
Direct emits no scores in the middle zone. An ensemble with a partner that does populate $[0.2, 0.8]$ creates ties within Direct's extreme clusters that the partner breaks in a signal-bearing way. v6's free-form JSON protocol naturally produces middle scores ($24$\%); v8's explicit refutation schema forces binary outcomes (all refuted $\to 0.05$ or surviving $\to 0.85$) so only $0.4$\% land in the middle. The ensemble gain on Qwen3.5 test correlates monotonically with middle-zone mass across our three ensembles. This suggests the next-generation refutation agent should emit \emph{graded} refutation-strength scores rather than a binary verdict---preserving the theoretical interpretability of v8 while capturing the ensemble benefit of v6.

\subsection{Per-domain behaviour.}
\label{ssec:perdomain_main}
The calibration router's choice coincides with the oracle on 9/12 domains for GPT-5.4 and 10/12 for both SeedVL and Qwen3.5. Mismatches concentrate where two strategies are within 1 pp on calibration (a bootstrap-level tie that the router cannot reliably resolve with 20 items), and the realised AUROC gap on test is small ($\le 0.06$). The per-backbone per-strategy per-domain table and the per-domain router-choice overlay are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain} (Figure~\ref{fig:per_domain_appendix}).

\subsection{Statistical significance}
\label{ssec:budget}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Stratified paired bootstrap summary (test split, $1{,}000$ resamples, per-domain stratification). $\Delta$ is the pairwise macro-AUROC gap; CI is the 95\% percentile interval; \checkmark\ means the CI excludes zero.}
\label{tab:bootstrap}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
Backbone & Comparison & $\Delta$ & 95\% CI & sig. \\
\midrule
GPT-5.4 & descriptor-router vs direct & $+0.017$ & $[+0.009, +0.024]$ & \checkmark \\
GPT-5.4 & calibration-router vs fusion & $-0.006$ & $[-0.022, +0.009]$ & --- \\
GPT-5.4 & fusion vs direct & $+0.015$ & $[+0.005, +0.025]$ & \checkmark \\
GPT-5.4 & oracle vs fusion & $+0.013$ & $[+0.001, +0.026]$ & \checkmark \\
\midrule
SeedVL & descriptor-router vs direct & $+0.022$ & $[+0.014, +0.031]$ & \checkmark \\
SeedVL & \textbf{calibration-router vs fusion} & \boldmath$+0.016$ & $[+0.000, +0.032]$ & \checkmark \\
SeedVL & fusion vs direct & $+0.016$ & $[+0.006, +0.027]$ & \checkmark \\
