    98	\end{tabular}
    99	\end{table}
   100	Direct emits no scores in the middle zone. An ensemble with a partner that does populate $[0.2, 0.8]$ creates ties within Direct's extreme clusters that the partner breaks in a signal-bearing way. v6's free-form JSON protocol naturally produces middle scores ($24$\%); v8's explicit refutation schema forces binary outcomes (all refuted $\to 0.05$ or surviving $\to 0.85$) so only $0.4$\% land in the middle. The ensemble gain on Qwen3.5 test correlates monotonically with middle-zone mass across our three ensembles. This suggests the next-generation refutation agent should emit \emph{graded} refutation-strength scores rather than a binary verdict---preserving the theoretical interpretability of v8 while capturing the ensemble benefit of v6.
   101	
   102	\subsection{Per-domain behaviour.}
   103	\label{ssec:perdomain_main}
   104	The calibration router's choice coincides with the oracle on 9/12 domains for GPT-5.4 and 10/12 for both SeedVL and Qwen3.5. Mismatches concentrate where two strategies are within 1 pp on calibration (a bootstrap-level tie that the router cannot reliably resolve with 20 items), and the realised AUROC gap on test is small ($\le 0.06$). The per-backbone per-strategy per-domain table and the per-domain router-choice overlay are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain} (Figure~\ref{fig:per_domain_appendix}).
   105	
   106	\subsection{Statistical significance}
   107	\label{ssec:budget}
   108	
   109	\begin{table}[t]
   110	\centering
   111	\caption{Stratified paired bootstrap summary (test split, $1{,}000$ resamples, per-domain stratification). $\Delta$ is the pairwise macro-AUROC gap; CI is the 95\% percentile interval; \checkmark\ means the CI excludes zero.}
   112	\label{tab:bootstrap}
   113	\small
   114	\begin{tabular}{llccc}
   115	\toprule
   116	Backbone & Comparison & $\Delta$ & 95\% CI & sig. \\
   117	\midrule
   118	GPT-5.4 & descriptor-router vs direct & $+0.017$ & $[+0.009, +0.024]$ & \checkmark \\
   119	GPT-5.4 & calibration-router vs fusion & $-0.006$ & $[-0.022, +0.009]$ & --- \\
   120	GPT-5.4 & fusion vs direct & $+0.015$ & $[+0.005, +0.025]$ & \checkmark \\
   121	GPT-5.4 & oracle vs fusion & $+0.013$ & $[+0.001, +0.026]$ & \checkmark \\
   122	\midrule
   123	SeedVL & descriptor-router vs direct & $+0.022$ & $[+0.014, +0.031]$ & \checkmark \\
   124	SeedVL & \textbf{calibration-router vs fusion} & \boldmath$+0.016$ & $[+0.000, +0.032]$ & \checkmark \\
   125	SeedVL & fusion vs direct & $+0.016$ & $[+0.006, +0.027]$ & \checkmark \\
   126	SeedVL & oracle vs calibration-router & $+0.013$ & $[+0.001, +0.025]$ & \checkmark \\
   127	\midrule
   128	Qwen3.5 & descriptor-router vs direct & $+0.049$ & $[+0.036, +0.061]$ & \checkmark \\
   129	Qwen3.5 & calibration-router vs fusion & $+0.001$ & $[-0.010, +0.011]$ & --- \\
   130	Qwen3.5 & fusion vs direct & $+0.055$ & $[+0.041, +0.070]$ & \checkmark \\
   131	Qwen3.5 & oracle vs calibration-router & $+0.014$ & $[+0.006, +0.022]$ & \checkmark \\
   132	\bottomrule
   133	\end{tabular}
   134	\end{table}
   135	
   136	Table~\ref{tab:bootstrap} summarises the stratified paired bootstrap for the headline comparisons. The calibration router strictly beats every single-strategy baseline on SeedVL (including fusion, significant); it ties fusion on GPT-5.4 and Qwen3.5 (fusion is the dominant single strategy on those backbones). Against the weaker \emph{direct} baseline that prior agentic-VAD work usually compares to, all three routers---descriptor, calibration, and oracle---show significant gains.
   137	
   138	\paragraph{Hyperparameter protocol and sensitivity.}
   139	All hyperparameters are frozen from the 20-item-per-domain calibration split and never re-tuned on dev/test: fusion weight $w{=}0.2$ (selected on GPT-5.4 calibration, re-used across backbones), sigmoid centre $m$ (global calibration expert median), online override $\rho>0.8$, descriptor-router rules (no metrics consulted), and calibration-router argmax. Leave-one-domain-out: the calibration-router--fusion gap is positive on 10/12 splits for SeedVL ($-0.8/+2.5/0.79$ pp min/max/std) and 9/12 for Qwen3.5; full table Appendix~\ref{app:loo}.
   140	
   141	\subsection{Negative results: why simpler agents fail}
   142	\label{ssec:ablation}
   143	
   144	We screened six alternative agent designs on the GPT-5.4 calibration split (20 items per domain, 240 items total) before arriving at the router. All uniformly regress the descriptor-enhanced direct baseline: self-refine $-5.9$ pp, naive debate $-7.2$ pp, gated debate $-4.1$ pp, evidence-injected proposer $-10.2$ pp, third-call arbiter $-9.1$ pp, normal-first $-2.4$ pp. The failure mode is consistent: an \emph{unconditional} second VLM call rationalises correct high-confidence predictions away. AnomalyClaw avoids this by (i) using an \emph{external} non-parametric expert as the escalation signal and (ii) using a router rather than a fixed sequence. Full variant table and per-variant failure analysis in Appendix~\ref{app:ablation}. These numbers should be read as \emph{design-screening evidence on one backbone's calibration split} rather than cross-backbone generalisation claims.
   145	
   146	\subsection{Complementarity and budget}
   147	\label{ssec:complementarity}
   148	The agent's maximum possible gain is bounded by how often different strategies disagree with ground truth in opposite directions. On the SeedVL test split, D5c liver CT and D4 dermoscopy have the largest expert-rescue rows (26\% / 21\% expert-only-correct over direct), while D5d GI and D6 LEVIR have large direct-only-correct rows (46\% / 38\%), matching the router's assignments. Average VLM budget for the calibration router is $\approx 1.0$ calls/image; the online $\rho$-override escalates $\sim 12\%$ of items, accounting for the whole gap to the 1.3-call bound. Full correctness matrix and routing distribution: Appendix~\ref{app:complementarity}, \ref{app:routing}.
   149	
   150	\subsection{Limitations}
   151	\label{ssec:limitations}
   152	The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom, suggesting a VLM-driven router reading image-level features may close more of the gap. Routing is calibrated from 20 items per domain; we evaluate a single expert backbone (DINOv2-giant) and a single frontier non-English VLM (SeedVL); the Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) remains at 60\% calibration accuracy on D9 and is not in the reported variant. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only. The online expert-signal override threshold ($\rho > 0.8$) is currently calibrated against patch-$k$-NN's normalised score; on industrial domains where SubspaceAD's raw distance is large, the override fires on essentially every \emph{normal}-predicted item and effectively reduces the strategy choice to ``fusion or interpret'' on those domains; a backbone-aware $\rho$ rescaling is left to future work.

